.TH "HTMLTABLEELEMENT.CREATETBODY" "3JS" "August 19, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
HTMLTableElement.createTBody \- HTMLTableElement: createTBody() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]createTBody()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]HTMLTableElement\f[R] interface creates a \f[CR]<tbody>\f[R]
element, inserts it immediately after the last \f[CR]<tbody>\f[R]
element child of the given \f[CR]<table>\f[R], or as the last child if
there is no such element, and returns it.
.PP
This method creates and inserts the element directly, without requiring
separate calls to methods such as \f[CR]Document.createElement()\f[R],
\f[CR]Node.insertBefore()\f[R], and \f[CR]Node.appendChild()\f[R].
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Unlike \f[CR]HTMLTableElement.createTHead()\f[R] and
\f[CR]HTMLTableElement.createTFoot()\f[R], \f[CR]createTBody()\f[R]
always creates a new \f[CR]<tbody>\f[R] element, even if the table
already contains one or more bodies.
.RE
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
createTBody()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
An \f[CR]HTMLTableSectionElement\f[R] (which is always a
\f[CR]tbody\f[R]).
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] myBody = myTable.createTBody();
\f[I]// Now this should be true: myBody === myTable.tBodies.item(myTable.tBodies.length \- 1)\f[R]
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]HTMLTableElement.createCaption()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]HTMLTableElement.createTFoot()\f[R]
